{{Okaiken topics}}

The '''constitutional history of Okaiken''' encompasses the drafting, amendment, and judicial interpretation of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]] since its enactment in 1970. The Constitution has been amended thirteen times, and its text bears the visible marks of each amendment—a palimpsest where the original 1970 provisions, the amendments, and the repeals are all legible.

For the current text of the Constitution, see [[Constitution of Okaiken]].

==Historical Context==

===The 1870 Saito Constitution===

{{Main|Saito Constitution}}

The 1970 Constitution replaced the '''Saito Constitution of 1870''', which was the foundational law of the [[Mitoyagi Empire]]. Drafted by [[Juichi Miyazaki]], the "Father of the Constitution," the Saito Constitution was granted by the Emperor and enshrined him as the supreme, divine source of all power. The Imperial Diet established under the Saito Constitution was subordinate to the Emperor, who could dissolve it at will and who commanded the military directly through the Imperial General Staff—free from civilian oversight.

The Saito Constitution's fatal flaw was its failure to establish civilian control of the military. By the 1930s, the military had begun to operate independently of the Diet, exploiting loopholes in the constitutional framework to pursue an expansionist agenda. The descent into authoritarianism culminated in the dissolution of all political parties into the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association]] (Taisei Yokusankai) and the nation's entry into the [[Great Adonian War]]. The war ended in 1952 with the Armistice of Okami, which left the nation's sovereignty intact but stripped it of overseas possessions and imposed a security pact with Echia.

===The Quiet Revolution and the Constitutional Question===

{{Main|Quiet Revolution}}

The [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1967–1968 was triggered by the fraudulent elections of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Socialist Party]] government under [[Genzui Kusaka]], who attempted to abolish the elected parliament and establish a one-party state. The revolution culminated in the intervention of [[Emperor Wakayama]], the defection of the Okami Capital Defense Force under General [[Ryohei Morita]], and the formation of the [[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party]] under [[Yoshimizu Ohno]].

The most complex issue facing the new government was the "Imperial Question." Emperor Wakayama's broadcast on June 12, 1968, had been the decisive act that prevented civil war, earning him immense public gratitude. However, his intervention also exposed the undemocratic power the Emperor still held under the Saito Constitution. In late 1968, Emperor Wakayama voluntarily offered to abdicate his political authority, stating that the Imperial role must evolve from a position of power to a "symbol of the people's unity and cultural heritage."

This voluntary surrender made the Republic possible. The 1970 Constitution was honest about what happened: it acknowledged the Emperor's role in preventing civil war, stripped him of all political power, and seized the vast private assets of the Imperial Household to fund the national recovery. The original Article 1 declared: "Sovereign power resides with the people of Okaiken. The Republic shall be governed by the elected representatives of the people, through the Diet, the Cabinet, and the Judiciary." The Emperor was not mentioned in the constitutional text except as a private citizen whose property had been seized under Article 8. The text never denied the Emperor's legitimacy—it simply removed his political authority.

The new Constitution was also shaped by the nation's experience in the Great Adonian War. Unlike many of the Allied powers, Okaiken did not lose the war; it surrendered after suffering catastrophic bombing but retained its sovereignty and was not occupied. The [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces|OSDF]] was established in 1954 under civilian control, replacing the dismantled Imperial military. The 1970 Constitution constitutionalized the OSDF, placing it under the Prime Minister's authority—a direct response to the Saito Constitution's failure to establish civilian control of the military.

===The Naming of the Nation===

{{Main|Etymology of Okaiken}}

The Constitution's original text referred to the nation as the "Federal Republic of Okaiken," but the name "Okaiken" itself was not formally adopted until July 12, 1971, under Executive Order 99. The name {{nihongo|Okaiken|岡井県}} combines {{nihongo|Oka|岡}}, meaning "hill" or "high ground"—the geographic origin of the capital, [[Okami]]—with {{nihongo|Ken|県}}, a legal term denoting "sovereign domain" or "administered country." The choice of ''Ken'' over the more conventional ''Koku'' (国, "nation") was deliberate: it encoded the technocratic, centralist values of the Republic, repurposing the Honda Shogunate's administrative vocabulary for democratic governance.

The people of the archipelago identify themselves as {{nihongo|'''Sanese'''|三人}}, a term derived from {{nihongo|Sankoku|三国}}, meaning "Three Lands," referencing the three primary islands of [[Harujima]], [[Ishijima]], and [[Yagikoshu]]. This archipelagic identity transcends any particular government or dynasty. The Constitution uses "Sanese people" (三人, ''Sanese-jin'') throughout—the nation is the administered domain; the people are the islanders.

===The Capital===

{{Main|Okami}}

The Constitution designates the capital as [[Okami]] (大紙, "Great Paper"), the historic metropolis on the island of [[Harujima]]. During the Republican era (1970–2011), the capital was officially renamed the "Central Metropolitan District" (中央広域区, ''Chūō Kōiki-ku'') as part of the Great Transformation's effort to sever the nation's ties to its feudal and imperial past. The 2011 [[Capital Restoration Act]], passed by the same referendum that restored the Emperor, reverted the name to Okami. The Constitution's current text reflects this reversion.

==Amendment History==

The Constitution of Okaiken has been amended thirteen times since its enactment in 1970. The amendments reflect the nation's political evolution: the early Republican era (Amendments 1–2), the social welfare expansion (Amendments 3–5), the security and economic crises (Amendments 6–8), the Lacashe settlement and its erosion (Amendments 9, 13), and the Jutsu Restoration (Amendments 10–12).

