===Amendments 1–2: The Name and the Symbol (1971)===

The First and Second Amendments were the product of Prime Minister [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]]'s reconciliation policy, which sought to heal the cultural wounds of the Quiet Revolution while preserving the Republic's constitutional structure.

'''First Amendment (1971): Name and Identity.''' The original 1970 text had referred to the nation as the "Federal Republic" without specifying the name "Okaiken." The name had been adopted by Executive Order 99 on July 12, 1971, but the constitutional text still used the generic "Federal Republic." The First Amendment constitutionalized the name "Okaiken" (岡井県) and the demonym "Sanese" (三人), and added the etymological explanation to the Preamble: the nation is the administered domain (''Ken'') of the hill (''Oka'') upon which the capital stands; the people are the islanders of the Three Lands (''Sankoku''). The referendum passed with 78.4%—the widest margin of any constitutional amendment—because it was uncontroversial: the name was already in use.

'''Second Amendment (1971): Symbolic Restoration.''' The original 1970 text had stripped the Emperor of all status, including symbolic status. The original Article 1 had established the Republic without reference to the Emperor: "Sovereign power resides with the people of Okaiken." The Emperor was not mentioned in the Constitution except as a private citizen whose property had been seized under Article 8. This was the most radical provision of the original text—and the most culturally alienating. Nakasone's amendment amended Article 1 to add: "The Emperor shall be the symbol of the Sanese identity, deriving his position from the will of the people with whom resides sovereign power. The Emperor shall have no political authority." The referendum passed with 71.6%, reflecting the public's gratitude for Emperor Wakayama's intervention in 1968 and their discomfort with the complete erasure of the Imperial House from the constitutional order.

===Amendment 3: Environmental Stewardship (1974)===

{{Main|Minamata Disease Litigation}}

The Third Amendment was a direct response to the environmental devastation of the Sanese Economic Miracle. The [[Minamata Disease Litigation]] (1971–1973), in which victims of industrial mercury poisoning sued the chemical conglomerates and the government that had enabled them, produced the political pressure for a constitutional guarantee. The amendment added Article 26A, imposing obligations on both the State and the people to maintain the archipelago's physical conditions as a trust for future generations.

The amendment was notable for its dual obligation structure: the State must prevent degradation and remediate harm; the people must not discharge pollutants beyond legal limits. This was the first constitutional provision to impose a direct duty on individual citizens, not just the state. It established the principle that constitutional rights and duties are reciprocal—the right to a healthy environment is paired with the duty not to degrade it.

The referendum passed with 74.2%, reflecting the broad public consensus that the Miracle's environmental costs had been too high.

===Amendment 4: Electoral Integrity (1976)===

The Fourth Amendment strengthened the Electoral Commission's independence and added the criminal penalties for electoral fraud directly into the Constitution. The original Chapter VIII had established the Commission but had given it limited audit powers and no criminal enforcement mechanism. The first post-revolution elections in 1973 exposed the gaps: the Commission could investigate fraud but could not void results, and the criminal penalties for fraud were defined by statute, not by the Constitution—meaning a future Kusaka could weaken them through ordinary legislation.

The amendment added Article 16A (connecting the right to petition to the emergency referendum process), Article 94 (the Commission's power to void results and order new elections), and Article 95 (criminal penalties for electoral fraud defined in the Constitution itself: five years' imprisonment, permanent disqualification from public office). The referendum passed with 81.3%—the highest margin of any constitutional amendment—because the public remembered the fraud that had triggered the Quiet Revolution and wanted it made structurally impossible.

===Amendment 5: Political Rights Restoration (1977)===

The Fifth Amendment repealed the 20-year ban on the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP) that had been imposed during the Quiet Revolution. The ban was set to expire in 1988, but the political realignment of 1977—which saw the dissolution of the Republican Party and the formation of the LDP and PPO—created an opportunity to end it early. The amendment also established the framework for party registration (Article 15A): political parties must register with the Electoral Commission, disclose their membership and financing, and are prohibited from advocating the violent overthrow of the constitutional order. No political party may be banned except by judicial order, following a finding that the party has violated these conditions.

The referendum passed with 62.7%—a comfortable margin but not an overwhelming one. The public was divided: many remembered the Kusaka government's attempt to abolish parliament, but others recognized that banning a political party was itself a form of authoritarianism.

===Amendment 6: Security Treaty (1979)===

{{Main|Treaty of Mutual Cooperation and Security (Okaiken–Echia)}}

The Sixth Amendment was the constitutional expression of the security treaty crisis of 1979. The revision of the Okaiken-Echia Mutual Security Assistance Pact had sparked the largest protests since the Quiet Revolution, forcing the resignation of Prime Minister [[Takeo Fukuda]]. The amendment constitutionalized the security pact but added a critical limitation: no Sanese military forces shall be deployed overseas for combat operations without the prior approval of the Diet, by a majority of two-thirds of each House (Article 9(4)).

The amendment was a compromise. The LDP wanted the security pact constitutionalized without limitations. The PPO and the Kōmeitō wanted the pact renegotiated or abandoned. The two-thirds Diet approval requirement was the middle ground: the pact was constitutional, but the government could not use it to deploy forces without broad legislative consensus.

The referendum passed with 58.9% on a turnout of 74.6%—the highest turnout of any constitutional referendum, reflecting the intensity of the public debate. The Sixth Amendment's deployment restriction would later be superseded by the Ninth Amendment (1984), which prohibited overseas combat deployment entirely.

===Amendment 7: Economic Sovereignty (1980)===

The Seventh Amendment was the constitutional foundation for [[Guided Capitalism]]. The Go-go 8090 era was beginning, and the government needed explicit constitutional authority for the informal mechanisms of economic coordination—administrative guidance (''gyōsei shidō''), amakudari (descent from heaven), and the keiretsu system—that had been operating on uncertain statutory footing. The amendment added Article 29(4) (state's power of administrative guidance, subject to judicial review) and Article 29(5) (corporate obligations proportional to incorporation privileges).

The amendment was controversial. Critics argued that administrative guidance was a form of state coercion that operated outside the rule of law—corporations "voluntarily" complied with government directives because the alternative was regulatory retaliation. The drafters responded by requiring judicial review: any administrative guidance that constituted "arbitrary deprivation of property or liberty" could be challenged in court. The {{nihongo|'''Administrative Guidance Case'''|行政指導裁判|Gyōsei Shidō Saiban}} (1985) upheld the amendment, holding that administrative guidance was constitutional so long as it was "genuinely voluntary and not the product of implicit coercion."

The referendum passed with 63.5%. The business community supported it; the labor movement was skeptical but did not oppose it strongly.

===Amendment 8: Public Service and National Harmony (1982)===

The Eighth Amendment was the most comprehensive single amendment to the Constitution. It added four new provisions:

* '''Article 12A:''' The Harmony Clause—rights must be exercised consistently with social harmony, but harmony cannot be used to suppress lawful dissent or enforce conformity.
* '''Article 26B:''' Community Policing—public order is a shared obligation of the State and the people; neighborhood associations may assist but not exercise police powers.
* '''Article 26C:''' National Service—all citizens must serve; conscientious objection recognized; Resilience Guarantee for those who complete service.
* '''Article 86(2):''' The Three Forges budget framework—no budget may allocate resources exclusively to one forge at the expense of the others.

The amendment was a product of the early bubble economy's social fragmentation. Rural communities were depopulating as young people migrated to the cities. The traditional mechanisms of social cohesion—the chōnaikai, the enterprise union, the lifetime employment system—were under strain. The LDP argued that the Constitution needed to articulate the reciprocal obligations that held the nation together. The PPO and Kōmeitō agreed, but insisted on the limits in Article 12A(2) and the conscientious objector clause in Article 26C(2).

The referendum passed with 59.1%—the narrowest margin of any Diet-initiated amendment. The Harmony Clause was the most controversial provision. Critics argued that "social harmony" was a blank check for the state to suppress dissent. The drafters' response—the explicit limits in Article 12A(2)—was intended to cabin the principle, but the subsequent case law has been divided.

===Amendment 9: Renunciation of War (1984)===

{{Main|Lacashe deployment}}

The Ninth Amendment was the most consequential amendment between the original Constitution and the Jutsu Restoration. It was a direct response to the [[Lacashe deployment]] (1981–1983), in which twelve Sanese personnel were killed during a multinational peacekeeping operation under the Echian security pact. The amendment replaced the original Chapter II (Military) with a new Chapter II (Renunciation of War), adding the three-paragraph Article 9 that prohibited war as a sovereign right, the maintenance of war potential, and the overseas deployment of Sanese military personnel for combat operations.

The PPO-Kōmeitō coalition under Prime Minister [[Tsutomu Hata]] drafted the Ninth Amendment, which was ratified by referendum on November 18, 1984, passing with 67.3% of the vote on a turnout of 71.2%.

Paragraph (3) was specific to Okaiken's experience. It did not merely renounce war—it specifically prohibited overseas combat deployment, referencing the Lacashe trauma directly. The Echian security cooperation agreement was renegotiated to reflect this new constitutional reality.

The amendment created a constitutional tension that would persist for thirty years: the original Articles 9–12 of the Military Chapter were repealed, but the new Article 9 said "land, sea, and air forces... will never be maintained" while the OSDF continued to exist. The Supreme Court resolved this tension in the {{nihongo|'''Article 9 Decisions'''|九条裁判|Kyūjō Saiban}} of 1997 and 2003, holding that the SDF's defensive posture and subordination to civilian authority distinguished it from the "war potential" that the Constitution forbade.

===Amendments 10–12: The Jutsu Restoration (2011)===

{{Main|Jutsu Restoration}}

The 2011 emergency referendum was the first invocation of Chapter IX. Three separate questions were put to the people on the same ballot on August 20, 2011:

'''Question 1: The Imperial Restoration''' — "Do you approve the amendment of Article 1 of the Constitution to restore the Emperor as Head of State (元首, Genshu)?" The question passed with 72.1% of the vote. The amendment replaced the Second Amendment's language ("symbol of the Sanese identity") with the current text: "The Emperor shall be the Head of State and the symbol of the unity of the People, deriving his position from the will of the people with whom resides sovereign power." The original 1970 text—"Sovereign power resides with the people of Okaiken. The Republic shall be governed by the elected representatives of the people"—remained as the foundation, with the Imperial language layered on top. The public remembered Emperor Wakayama's intervention in 1968. The restoration of the Emperor as Head of State was the easiest question—it was about dignity, not power.

'''Question 2: The Capital Restoration''' — "Do you approve the Capital Restoration Act, reverting the name of the capital from the Central Metropolitan District to Okami?" The question passed with 64.3% of the vote. The name "Okami" had never stopped being used in common speech. The "Central Metropolitan District" was always a Republican imposition. The reversion was popular but not overwhelming—some younger voters saw it as nostalgic symbolism.

'''Question 3: The Sovereignty Act''' — "Do you approve the constitutionalization of the Sovereignty Act, granting the Emperor reserve powers to: (a) dissolve the Diet when it cannot pass a budget within 120 days; (b) designate a Prime Minister when the Diet cannot agree within 30 days; and (c) resolve budgetary deadlocks as defined by law?" The question passed with '''54.2%''' of the vote—the narrowest margin of any constitutional question in Okaiken's history. The opposition—the PPO, the Kōmeitō, and the remnants of the Republican old guard—fought this question hard. They argued that the Sovereignty Act was the first step back to the Imperial absolutism that the Quiet Revolution had overthrown.

The Sovereignty Act passed because the 120-Day Shutdown was still fresh. The public had lived through four months of government paralysis. They wanted a mechanism to prevent it from happening again. But the narrow margin meant the mandate was contested—and that contestation would shape the next decade of Sanese politics.

[[Emperor Masahito]]'s televised address on April 20, 2011—in which he chastised the Jōhei cabinet for failing to "heat the homes of his people"—served as the campaign for all three questions. The Emperor did not explicitly endorse the amendments, but the implication was clear: the system that had stripped the Emperor of all political power had produced a government that couldn't govern.

===Amendment 13: The Kurosawa Doctrine (2014)===

{{Main|Kurosawa Doctrine}}

The 2014 amendment to Article 9 was the most controversial constitutional change since the Jutsu Restoration. It was the product of a two-stage process: first, a Cabinet reinterpretation under Prime Minister [[Toshiro Kurosawa]]; then, a formal amendment approved by referendum.

In May 2013, the Kurosawa government issued a Cabinet resolution reinterpreting Article 9 to permit "collective self-defense," arguing that the protection of Okaiken's assets and allies was consistent with the article's defensive purpose. The opposition challenged the reinterpretation, and the Supreme Court issued a split ruling in the {{nihongo|'''Collective Self-Defense Case'''|集団的自衛権裁判|Shūdanteki Jieiken Saiban}} (2013), holding by a vote of 8–7 that the reinterpretation was "not inconsistent" with Article 9—a narrow, technical ruling that did not endorse the reinterpretation but did not strike it down.

The 8–7 ruling was a watershed. The narrow margin reflected the deep division within the Court and the nation. The ruling also intensified the political pressure on [[Sasuke Genba]], who had preceded Kurosawa as Prime Minister and who opposed the erosion of Article 9. Genba's suicide on February 15, 2013, was driven by multiple factors—the LDP's corruption, the Emperor's expanding influence, and a vote of no confidence he could not survive—but the prospect of Article 9's weakening was among the final blows. The person who would have defended the Lacashe settlement was gone.

Kurosawa used the Supreme Court's ruling as political cover to push the formal amendment through the normal Article 105 process (two-thirds of both houses plus referendum). The amendment was ratified by referendum on October 26, 2014, passing with 56.1% of the vote on a turnout of 58.7%—the narrowest margin for a Diet-initiated amendment.

The amendment repealed paragraph (2) of Article 9 ("land, sea, and air forces... will never be maintained") and replaced paragraph (3) with the authorization to maintain forces "to protect Okaiken's most valuable assets." The prohibition on overseas combat deployment was also removed, replaced by the vague authorization to "protect assets and allies."

==Repealed Provisions and Structural Gaps==

The Constitution of Okaiken is a palimpsest: the original 1970 text, the amendments, and the repeals are all visible in the document's structure. The most significant structural gaps are the product of the Ninth Amendment (1984), which repealed the original Chapter II without fully replacing it.

===Original Articles 9–12 (1970): Repeled by the Ninth Amendment (1984)===

The original Chapter II contained four articles:

* '''Article 9 (Original):''' The Prime Minister as Commander-in-Chief of the Self-Defense Forces.
* '''Article 10 (Original):''' The Self-Defense Forces as a permanent institution of the State, under civilian control.
* '''Article 11 (Original):''' Prohibition on military independence; the Imperial General Staff model permanently prohibited.
* '''Article 12 (Original):''' Prohibition on domestic deployment for suppression of dissent.

The Ninth Amendment replaced all four articles with a single three-paragraph Article 9. The new Article 9 renounced war, prohibited the maintenance of forces, and prohibited overseas combat deployment—but it did not restate the civilian control provisions, the prohibition on military independence, or the prohibition on domestic suppression. These provisions were effectively deleted from the constitutional text.

The consequences of this deletion have been significant:

* '''Civilian control of the military''' is now governed by statute (the Self-Defense Forces Act of 1954, as amended), not by the Constitution. The Prime Minister's authority as Commander-in-Chief is a statutory, not constitutional, provision. The Supreme Court has upheld this arrangement, but it means that a future government could weaken civilian control through ordinary legislation—something that was not possible under the original Articles 9–12.
* '''The prohibition on military independence''' (original Article 11) has no constitutional successor. The Self-Defense Forces Act prohibits the establishment of an independent military command, but this is a statutory prohibition, not a constitutional one.
* '''The prohibition on domestic suppression''' (original Article 12) has been partially reconstructed through the Community Policing provision (Article 26B, Eighth Amendment 1982), which prohibits military organizations from exercising police powers. But Article 26B is narrower than the original Article 12.

===Article 9(2) (1984): Repealed by the Thirteenth Amendment (2014)===

The Ninth Amendment's paragraph (2)—"land, sea, and air forces, as well as other war potential, will never be maintained"—was repealed by the 2014 amendment. This repeal removed the prohibition on maintaining forces but did not restore the original Articles 10–12. The current Article 9, paragraph (3), authorizes the maintenance of forces "to protect Okaiken's most valuable assets," but it does not specify the conditions of civilian control, the prohibition on military independence, or the limits on domestic deployment.

===Article 9(4) (1979): Superseded by the Ninth Amendment (1984)===

The Sixth Amendment (1979) had added Article 9(4), requiring a two-thirds Diet vote for any overseas combat deployment. The Ninth Amendment (1984) prohibited overseas combat deployment entirely, rendering paragraph (4) moot. The 2014 amendment (Thirteenth Amendment) did not restore paragraph (4), meaning that the current Constitution has no explicit provision requiring legislative approval for overseas deployment—a gap that the Kurosawa government exploited when it issued the Cabinet resolution on collective self-defense in 2013.

===The Structural Pattern===

Every amendment to Chapter II has removed constitutional constraints on the military without replacing them with equivalent provisions. The original 1970 text had the most robust civilian control framework in Fosian constitutional law. The Ninth Amendment (1984) deleted it in favor of pacifism. The Thirteenth Amendment (2014) deleted the pacifism provisions in favor of collective self-defense. The result is a Constitution that authorizes military force but provides fewer constitutional constraints on that force than the original 1970 text.

==Major Legislation Under the Constitution==

The constitutional provisions have been given force through a body of legislation that reflects the political pendulum of Okaiken's post-revolutionary history.

===Progressive Legislation===

These laws were enacted under the authority of the constitutional provisions that impose obligations on the state—the right to resilience (Art. 25(3)), the environmental stewardship duty (Art. 26A), the national service obligation (Art. 26C), and the Electoral Commission's authority (Ch. VIII).

* '''National Nursing Care Act (1996):''' Enacted under Article 25(3). A major expansion of the welfare state for Okaiken's aging population.
* '''Youth Entertainment Regulation Act (YERA, 2005):''' Prohibited exclusive contracts for minors, banned appearance control clauses, abolished no-dating provisions. Enacted under Article 27(3) and the Guardian Standard framework.
* '''Idol Liberation Act (ILA, 2010):''' Dismantled vertically integrated agency monopolies, imposed 15% market concentration limits, mandated broadcasting-agency separation. Enacted under Article 29(5).
* '''Digital Rights Act (2017):''' Established strict judicial oversight requirements for government use of artificial intelligence. Enacted in direct response to the Satori Surveillance Case (2016), under Article 21.
* '''National Universal Health Act (2023):''' Established a single-payer healthcare system. Enacted under Article 25(3).
* '''National Iron Body Initiative (NIBI, 2019):''' Established Iron Body Centers in every prefecture, providing free physical training facilities, martial arts instruction, and health screening. Enacted under Article 25(3) and Article 26C.
* '''Criminal Rehabilitation Act (1972, as amended):''' Implemented the rehabilitation and reintegration framework of Article 40A. Established graduated sentencing, parole review, and post-release reintegration support. Enacted under Article 31 and Article 40A.

===Pro-Business Legislation===

These laws were enacted under the authority of the constitutional provisions that authorize state coordination of economic activity—the administrative guidance power (Art. 29(4)), the digital sovereignty requirement (Art. 29(4)), and the corporate obligations framework (Art. 29(5)).

* '''Self-Defense Forces Act (1954, as amended):''' Established the OSDF under civilian control. Originally enacted under the original Articles 9–12; now governed by statute following the repeal of those articles.
* '''Administrative Guidance Act (1981):''' Codified the procedures and limits of administrative guidance, establishing the requirement for written guidance, the right of recipients to refuse, and the judicial review standard. Enacted under Article 29(4).
* '''Digital Infrastructure Act (2008):''' Shifted the nation's industrial strategy from manufacturing to digital sovereignty. Created the Yagikoshu Data Corridor, subsidized Kizan Systems. Enacted under Article 29(4).
* '''Income Doubling Plan (1963, constitutionalized 1980):''' The signature economic policy of the Honda era, constitutionalized by the Seventh Amendment's administrative guidance provisions.

===Conservative Legislation===

These laws were enacted under the authority of the constitutional provisions that authorize the state to maintain order and enforce social cohesion—the harmony clause (Art. 12A), the community policing provision (Art. 26B), and the national service obligation (Art. 26C).

* '''National Service Act (1983):''' Implemented the national service obligation of Article 26C. Established the three tracks (military, civil defense, community service), the Resilience Guarantee, and the conscientious objector process.
* '''Community Safety Organizations Act (1983):''' Implemented the community policing provisions of Article 26B. Defined the authority and limits of chōnaikai and other community safety organizations.
* '''Workplace Expression Protection Guidelines (2013):''' Prohibits adverse employment actions based on gender expression or relationship status in public institutions and NIBI-registered facilities. A limited measure—no comprehensive national law exists. Enacted under Article 14.
* '''Companion Registration Act (2014):''' Established the legal framework for Companion Registration, conferring rights equivalent to marriage upon registered companions. Rooted in the nenja tradition. Enacted under Article 24.

==Key Judicial Decisions==

{{Main|Supreme Court of Okaiken}}

The [[Supreme Court of Okaiken]] has played a central role in interpreting the Constitution and resolving the tensions produced by its amendment history. Key decisions include:

* '''{{nihongo|Common Welfare Doctrine|共同の福祉論|Kyōdō no Fukushi Ron}} (1972):''' Established that the Court may assess not only the formal compliance of a law with the Constitution, but also the structural effects of the law—whether the law, as applied, produces outcomes inconsistent with the Constitution's principles. The original decision used the term {{nihongo|"public welfare"|公共の福祉|Kōkyō no Fukushi}}, but the doctrine was subsequently reframed under the "common welfare" standard following the constitutional shift from "public welfare" to "common welfare" in the amended text.
* '''Electoral Apportionment Decision (1976):''' Declared that severe malapportionment of Diet electoral districts violated Article 14 and Article 15, establishing the "one person, one vote" principle.
* '''Kusaka Purge Case (1990):''' Held that the 1968 emergency decree dissolving the Socialist Party was unconstitutional ''ab initio'', establishing that even revolutionary governments are bound by constitutional limits.
* '''Article 9 Decisions (1997, 2003):''' Upheld the constitutionality of the Self-Defense Forces under Article 9, holding that the SDF's defensive posture distinguished it from the "war potential" the Constitution forbade.
* '''Administrative Guidance Case (1985):''' Upheld the Seventh Amendment's administrative guidance provisions, holding that guidance was constitutional so long as it was "genuinely voluntary and not the product of implicit coercion."
* '''Chōnaikai Surveillance Case (1988):''' Held that a neighborhood association's monitoring of a resident violated Article 12A(2), because the association had invoked "harmony" to enforce conformity of lifestyle.
* '''Workplace Dress Code Case (1995):''' Upheld a corporate dress code prohibiting male employees from wearing makeup, holding that the code was a "reasonable regulation of workplace harmony."
* '''Kuramoto v. Okami Broadcasting (2012):''' Held that the YERA and ILA could not be "weaponized against performers who choose an androgynous presentation."
* '''Sovereignty Act Case (2012):''' Confirmed that the Emperor's reserve powers are constitutional but can only be exercised under the specific conditions defined in Articles 1A and 1B.
* '''Satori Surveillance Case (2016):''' Ruled 10–5 that the Satori AI surveillance program violated Articles 21 and 35. Triggered the collapse of the Kurosawa government.
* '''Collective Self-Defense Case (2013):''' Held 8–7 that the Cabinet's reinterpretation of Article 9 was "not inconsistent" with the Constitution.
* '''Marriage Equality Decision (2021):''' Held that the government's refusal to recognize same-sex marriages violated Article 14 and Article 24.
* '''Kurosawa Doctrine Review (2022):''' Upheld the 2014 amendment to Article 9 against a procedural challenge.

The '''Advocate General''' (Article 82A, added by legislative amendment) serves as an independent constitutional officer with the duty to represent the public interest before the courts. The Advocate General may bring constitutional actions before the Supreme Court, intervene in cases involving constitutional rights, and issue reports on matters of public interest. The office reports annually to the Diet on the state of constitutional rights in Okaiken.

